X["findreplace"]=[
 ["Worked example: updating a price list for the new year",
  "A café has a two-page PDF menu exported years ago; the designer is gone and only the PDF remains. Prices are unchanged, but the footer says *Menu 2025* on both pages and the phone number changed. Drop the PDF, type *2025* in Find and *2026* in Replace, then add one line in the extra pairs box: *0161 496 0000 => 0161 496 0999*. Keep Match case on and press the button. The result reports, for example, *4 replacements*: two years and two phone numbers. Open the file, zoom in on the footer and you will see the new text sitting exactly where the old one was, in a matching size. Print a test page; the white cover boxes are invisible on the white paper background.",
  "The same approach works for a training handout that names last year's trainer, a product sheet with an old model number or a certificate template where *[NAME]* needs replacing with a real name."],
 ["Worked example: anonymising a sample document",
  "Suppose you want to share a sample report with a supplier but replace the client name *Northwind Traders* with *Client A*. Find *Northwind Traders*, replace with *Client A*, and add a second pair for the short form, *Northwind => Client A*. Order matters: the longer phrase is listed first so it is replaced as a whole before the shorter word is searched. Because covered text is still present underneath, this is fine for a casual sample but not for genuine confidentiality – for that, redact the names with [Redact PDF](/redact-pdf/), which removes them from the file entirely, and then add replacement labels if needed."],
 ["Step-by-step troubleshooting",
  {"table":[["Problem","Likely cause","Fix"],
   ["“Text not found”","The PDF is a scan, or the phrase spans two text runs","Try a shorter word; run OCR on scans"],
   ["Only some occurrences replaced","Different capitalisation","Turn Match case off"],
   ["New text overlaps the next word","Replacement is longer than the original","Shorten it or use an abbreviation"],
   ["White rectangle visible","Page background is coloured or an image","Edit that spot manually in Edit PDF"],
   ["Replacement looks bolder/thinner","Original used a different font weight","Usually acceptable; edit headlines manually"],
   ["Strange characters in original","The PDF font has no Unicode mapping","Search for the visible text using OCR output"]]}],
 ["Why some PDFs cannot be searched",
  "Finding text depends on the PDF containing a text layer with a proper mapping from glyphs to characters. Scanned documents and photos converted to PDF contain only images: there is nothing to search until you run [OCR PDF](/ocr-pdf/), which recognises the characters and adds an invisible text layer. Some PDFs created by older design or printing software embed fonts without a Unicode map, so copying text produces gibberish; search fails on those too. A quick test: open the PDF in your browser, press Ctrl+F and search for the word. If the viewer finds it, this tool will almost always find it as well."],
 ["Case sensitivity, whole words and partial matches",
  "With *Match case* on, *Ltd* does not match *LTD* or *ltd*. Turn it off to catch every variant – but be aware that the replacement is written exactly as you typed it. The search matches text inside longer words too: replacing *cat* would also change *category* and *concatenate*. To avoid this, include surrounding characters, such as a space or punctuation (*cat.* or *cat *), or use a longer, unique phrase. When in doubt, check the replacement count against what you expect."],
 ["Keeping the layout tidy",
  "Because PDFs do not reflow, the best results come from replacements that are about the same width as the original. Digits, dates and codes are easy. Names of different lengths need a little care: *Jo Smith → Jonathan Smithson* will run past the original space. Options are to abbreviate, to use a slightly smaller wording, or to fix that one place by hand in [Edit PDF](/edit-pdf/), where you can move and resize text boxes freely. For whole paragraphs that need rewriting, convert with [PDF to Word](/pdf-to-word/) instead."],
 ["Find and replace on a phone or tablet",
  "The tool runs in mobile browsers, including Safari on iPhone and iPad and Chrome on Android. Typing pairs on a phone is easiest in the main boxes; for several pairs, paste a prepared list into the extra pairs box. Processing happens on the device, so very large PDFs (hundreds of pages) take longer on older phones. The download is saved to Files or Downloads and can be shared straight from there."],
 ["Privacy and how your file is processed",
  "Many online PDF editors upload the whole document to a server, process it there and keep it for a period. Here, the PDF is opened by JavaScript in your own browser tab. Text extraction uses the open-source PDF.js library; the edited PDF is written with pdf-lib. Nothing is sent to a server, nothing is stored, and you can even disconnect from the internet after the page has loaded and the tool still works. Closing the tab clears the document from memory."],
 ["Checklist before you send an edited PDF",
  {"list":["Compare the replacement count with the number of occurrences you expected.",
   "Zoom to 200% on two or three changed spots and check alignment.",
   "Search the output for the old text with Ctrl+F – covered text may still be found; if that matters, redact instead.",
   "Print one page if the document is going to be printed.",
   "Keep the original PDF unchanged in case you need to redo the edit.",
   "If the document is signed, remember that any edit invalidates the digital signature."]}],
 ["Digital signatures and form fields",
  "Editing a digitally signed PDF changes its content, so the signature will show as invalid in viewers – which is the purpose of a signature. Make the edits before signing, or ask the signer for an updated version. Form field values are not page text and are not changed by this tool; to change them, open the form in [Fill PDF form](/fill-pdf-form/), or flatten the form first with [Flatten PDF](/flatten-pdf/) to turn field values into regular page text that can be found."],
 ["Find and replace vs other editing options",
  {"table":[["Goal","Best approach"],["Same change in many places","Find and replace (this page)"],["One-off edit, move or restyle text","[Edit PDF](/edit-pdf/)"],["Rewrite paragraphs","PDF to Word, edit, Word to PDF"],["Remove sensitive information","[Redact PDF](/redact-pdf/)"],["Add new text everywhere, like a header","[Add header and footer](/pdf-header-footer/)"],["Check what changed","[Compare PDF](/compare-pdf/)"]]}]]
X["flatten"]=[["Worked example","A completed job application form has fields that look different on every viewer and can be changed by anyone. Drop it here, flatten, and send the result: the answers now look identical everywhere, print reliably, and can no longer be edited by mistake. Keep your fillable original in case you need to update an answer later."]]
X["grayscale"]=[["Worked example","A 30-page colour report costs a lot to print in colour. Convert it here with *Print* quality to get crisp grey text and images at 200 dpi, or choose *B&W* with the default threshold for pure black text on white paper – ideal for laser printers and for faxes. Compare file sizes in the result message."]]
X["deskew"]=[["Worked example","You scanned a 10-page contract on a sheet-fed scanner and pages 3 and 7 came out tilted by a couple of degrees. Drop the PDF here: the tool measures the angle of the text lines on each page and rotates only the pages that need it. Pages already straight are left as they are. Check the preview, and if a page still looks off, rotate it manually with [Rotate PDF](/rotate-pdf/)."],
 ["Tips for straighter scans","Place pages flush against the scanner glass edge, use the document feeder guides, and scan at 200–300 dpi. Good contrast helps the angle detection: avoid very light pencil or faint copies. Deskew before running OCR, as recognition is more accurate on straight lines."]]
X["redact"]=[["Worked example","Before sharing a bank statement with a landlord, tick *Auto-redact e-mail addresses* and *phone numbers*, add your account number as search text, and press Redact. Every match is covered with a solid box and removed from the text layer, so searching the output for the number finds nothing."]]
X["compare"]=[["Worked example","A supplier sends back a revised contract and says only the payment terms changed. Drop the original and the revision: highlighted differences show exactly which words were added or removed on each page, so you can confirm that nothing else – a liability cap, a date, a notice period – was quietly altered. Save the comparison for your records."],
 ["Tips for accurate comparisons","Compare text-based PDFs for word-level results; run [OCR](/ocr-pdf/) on scans first. If pages were inserted, differences after that point may be shifted – compare sections with [Extract pages](/extract-pages-from-pdf/) when needed."]]
X["bates"]=[["Worked example","A paralegal must produce three PDFs – 120, 300 and 114 pages – as one numbered set with prefix SMITH. Drop the files in order, set prefix SMITH-, start 1, digits 6, and press the button. The result reports SMITH-000001 – SMITH-000534; the second file starts at SMITH-000121 and the third at SMITH-000421. Record this range in the production log."]]
X["flatten"].append(["When not to flatten","Do not flatten a form that someone else still needs to fill in, or a document you will edit again. Flattening is a final step, like printing to paper: do it on a copy, just before sending or archiving."])
X["compare"].append(["What the comparison does not cover","Text is compared word by word; changes to images, colours, fonts and layout are not highlighted. For visual changes, open both files side by side, or convert pages with [PDF to JPG](/pdf-to-jpg/) and inspect them. Metadata and hidden content are not compared either."])
X["deskew"][1][1]+=" Very skewed pages, beyond about 10 degrees, are best rescanned."
X["bates"].append(["Before you number","Put files in their final order, remove duplicates, and apply redactions first, because numbers should be the very last change."])
X["findreplace"]+=[
 ["Worked example: filling a certificate template",
  "Training providers often keep a single certificate PDF with placeholders such as *[NAME]*, *[COURSE]* and *[DATE]*. To produce one certificate, enter *[NAME]* → *Priya Sharma* in the main boxes and add two pairs: *[COURSE] => First Aid at Work* and *[DATE] => 25 October 2026*. Because placeholders are written in square brackets, they never clash with ordinary words, so you can safely turn Match case off. Save the result under the participant's name and repeat for the next person, always starting from the clean template. For many certificates, keep a text file with the pairs ready to paste."],
 ["Who uses find and replace in PDF",
  {"list":["**Small businesses** updating menus, price lists, catalogues and flyers without a designer.",
   "**Administrators** correcting names, dates and reference numbers in letters and notices.",
   "**Teachers** reusing worksheets and exam papers with a new date or class name.",
   "**Event organisers** changing venue details or times on posters and programmes.",
   "**Freelancers** reusing proposals and quotes for a new client.",
   "**Job seekers** fixing a typo in a CV when the Word version is lost."]}],
 ["Good habits for reliable replacements",
  "Write down exactly what you want to change before you start, including variants: *Ltd*, *Ltd.*, *Limited*. Search for the most specific phrases first and the general ones last. Keep replacements in the same style as the original – if a heading is in capitals, type the replacement in capitals. Use unique placeholders in your own templates so that future replacements are effortless. And always keep the untouched original next to the edited copy; since every run starts from the file you drop, you can try again as often as you like until the result is perfect.",
  "If the document will be edited regularly, it is worth recreating it once in an editable format – for example via [PDF to Word](/pdf-to-word/) – and exporting fresh PDFs from that source in future. Find and replace is the quick fix; a maintained source file is the long-term solution."]]
X["compare"][-1][1]+=" Use it as a fast first check, then review key clauses yourself."
X["deskew"][1][1]+=" Straight pages also look more professional when shared."
X["findreplace"]+=[
 ["Understanding the result message",
  "After processing, the tool tells you how many replacements were made across the document. If you listed several pairs, the total combines them. A count of zero means none of the search texts were found – check spelling, try Match case off, or search for a shorter fragment. A count higher than expected usually means the search text appears inside other words; make it more specific and run again on the original file. The edited PDF keeps the same page count, page size, images, links and bookmarks as the original; only the matched text areas change."],
 ["Languages and special characters",
  "Search works with any language whose text is stored as Unicode in the PDF, including accented European languages, Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic and many Asian scripts. Replacement text in non-Latin scripts is drawn using a Unicode font that loads automatically when needed. Right-to-left text and complex scripts that join letters are matched as stored in the PDF, which can differ from how they appear; if a phrase is not found, try searching for a single word. Ligatures in some fonts (for example *fi* drawn as one glyph) can also prevent matches – search for a part of the word that avoids them."],
 ["Is find and replace in PDF reliable enough for business documents?",
  "For short, factual changes – years, prices, names, phone numbers, references – yes, provided you review the result. It is a visual edit that leaves the rest of the page exactly as it was, so there is no risk of the layout being rebuilt or fonts being substituted across the whole document, which can happen when converting to Word and back. For legal or regulated documents, treat the edited PDF as a new version: record what changed and, where required, have it re-approved or re-signed."]]
X["findreplace"].append(["Quick reference",
 {"table":[["Want to…","Do this"],["Change one word everywhere","Find + Replace boxes"],["Change several things","Add old => new lines"],["Delete a word","Leave Replace empty"],["Catch all capitalisations","Match case off"],["Avoid changing parts of words","Include a space or punctuation"],["Replace in a scan","OCR PDF first"],["Remove sensitive text for good","Redact PDF"],["Check the edits","Compare PDF with the original"]]},
 "Keep this table in mind and most edits take under a minute: drop, type, check the count, download, review. If the first attempt is not perfect, adjust the search text and run again on the untouched original – nothing is lost, and nothing ever leaves your device."])
X["findreplace"][-1][2]+=" For recurring jobs, save your list of pairs in a text file so the same set of corrections can be pasted and applied to every new version of the document."
